TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "lineup coverage",
    "structural roles",
    "organizer",
    "shaper",
    "terminal",
    "talent",
    "player counts",
    "within-team variation"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "how to define/measure structural roles",
    "how to quantify lineup coverage",
    "how to demonstrate separability from talent",
    "how to control for talent",
    "how to measure within-team variation",
    "what constitutes 'talent control behaves'"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No historical evidence was retrieved, so nothing is established or refuted by prior work regarding this specific research question."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The core challenge lies in the operational definition and assignment of 'organizer', 'shaper', and 'terminal' roles to players using the available play-by-play and derived play-type data. If these roles can be reliably assigned, the rest of the question appears amenable to statistical analysis.",
    "The phrase 'talent control behaves' likely refers to the expectation that talent, when controlled for, will have a predictable relationship with performance, and that structural role effects should remain independent of talent."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The precise definitions of 'organizer', 'shaper', and 'terminal' roles within the project context are unknown without access to a glossary or explicit definitions.",
    "The specific outcome variable for which 'separability from talent' is to be demonstrated is not stated in the research question. This could refer to lineup characteristics or performance outcomes.",
    "The precise operationalization of 'talent' and 'player counts' needs to be defined for the analysis.",
    "The exact meaning of 'talent control behaves' is ambiguous and requires a clear operational definition for testing."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No historical evidence was available to inform or change the decision."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.6,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "The definitions and reliable assignment of 'organizer', 'shaper', and 'terminal' roles are critical and not explicitly provided. If these roles cannot be reliably derived from the available data, the question becomes untestable."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "While total possessions are high, specific combinations of lineup coverage (e.g., exact counts of each role) might be rare, especially for 'within-team variation' analysis, leading to insufficient sample sizes for certain comparisons."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No prior work directly addressing this specific research question was retrieved."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "The assignment of 'organizer', 'shaper', and 'terminal' roles from play-by-play data is complex and prone to measurement error or lack of clear project-specific definitions. This could lead to unreliable role classifications."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Lineup composition, and thus structural role coverage, can be confounded by coaching decisions, opponent strength, game state, and player availability (injuries, rest). Controlling for all relevant confounders will be challenging."
    },
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "Analyzing 'within-team variation' for specific lineup coverage configurations might suffer from low power if certain combinations are infrequent, especially when controlling for multiple factors like talent."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "Some specific combinations of structural role coverage (e.g., highly unusual distributions of organizers, shapers, and terminals) might be extremely rare, making it difficult to draw statistically significant conclusions about their impact or separability."
    },
    {
      "code": "UNCERTAINTY",
      "text": "The phrase 'talent control behaves' is vague. If not precisely operationalized, it could lead to an untestable or unfalsifiable component of the research question."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.3,
    "rejected": 0.2,
    "untestable": 0.4,
    "artifact": 0.05,
    "uncertain": 0.05
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The research will test three main hypotheses: (1) Lineup coverage (counts of players assigned 'organizer', 'shaper', and 'terminal' roles) varies significantly within teams. (2) This lineup coverage is separable from 'talent' (defined as player-level Box Plus/Minus (BPM) or similar aggregate efficiency metric) and simple 'player counts' (e.g., guards, forwards, centers), meaning that role coverage explains additional variation in lineup characteristics or performance beyond talent and player counts. (3) The relationship between talent and lineup performance is consistent with established basketball analytics when structural roles are considered.",
    "unit": "The primary unit of analysis will be the possession. For 'within-team variation' and 'separability' analyses, aggregated lineup segments (e.g., full rotation intervals, or fixed-duration lineup stints) within a game will also be used as units.",
    "sample": "NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and 2025-26 (partial) regular seasons and playoffs. All 420,993 possessions and corresponding lineup data will be included. Only lineups with at least one player for each of organizer, shaper, and terminal roles will be considered for the 'coverage' aspect, if this is a strict interpretation of '>=1 organizer, shaper, terminal'. Otherwise, all observed lineups will be included, and role counts will be treated as variables.",
    "power": "Power analysis will be conducted after initial role assignment and data aggregation to determine the minimum detectable effect size for within-team variation and separability claims, given the observed distribution of lineup coverages and talent. A significance level of alpha = 0.05 will be used.",
    "controls": "Control variables for talent will include individual player-level BPM (Box Plus/Minus) for each player in the lineup, or a lineup-level aggregate (e.g., sum or average BPM). Player counts will control for traditional positional breakdowns (guards, forwards, centers) or simple counts of players in the lineup. Additional controls will include opponent strength (e.g., opponent's season winning percentage), game context (home/away, score differential, quarter/period), and coach identity.",
    "falsification": "The 'within-team variation' hypothesis would be falsified if a statistical test (e.g., ANOVA or mixed-effects model with team random effects) shows no significant variation in role coverage distributions within teams. 'Separability' would be falsified if, after controlling for talent and player counts, structural role coverage variables do not significantly explain additional variation in lineup characteristics or performance (e.g., offensive rating, effective field goal percentage). 'Talent control behaves' would be falsified if the coefficients for talent variables are inconsistent with established understanding or if significant interaction effects between talent and structural roles mask primary effects.",
    "multiplicity": "Given multiple hypotheses (variation, separability, talent control), a Bonferroni correction or similar adjustment will be applied to p-values to control the family-wise error rate, if multiple independent tests are performed. Alternatively, a single hierarchical model can be used to test all components simultaneously.",
    "decision_rule": "The research will 'proceed' if all three main hypotheses (variation, separability, and talent control behavior) are supported at p < 0.05 after multiplicity correction and methodological risks are adequately addressed. If any hypothesis is rejected or deemed untestable due to data limitations or methodological constraints (e.g., inability to reliably assign roles), the decision will be 'do not proceed' with the full scope of the question, or 'proceed with changes' focusing only on testable components.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 regular season and playoffs, NBA 2023-24 regular season and playoffs, NBA 2024-25 regular season and playoffs, NBA 2025-26 regular season (complete from 2026-04-13), NBA 2025-26 playoffs (complete from 2026-06-21). Derived play-type possession records and NBA stats API data as described."
  }
}

